    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The geologic history of the basinal sequence of marls and calcarenites from the northern Maiella Mountain records the development of multiple generations and types of structures. In this paper, we concentrate on the strike-slip faults that formed during the later part of the mountain’s history and we recognize the mechanisms and hierarchy of structures that lead to fault formation and evolution. The fundamental structures, that are the building blocks for fault development, are pressure solution seams.\r\n\r\nThe following steps describe the mechanism for strike-slip fault development: 1. Formation of a set of pressure solution seams associated with bedding parallel slip. 2. Slip is resolved on the pre-existing pressure solution seams and pressure solution tails develop. Connections of faults occur preferentially at left steps between sheared pressure solution segments, for right lateral faults, and at right steps for left lateral faults. 3. With increasing slip, sheared pressure solution seams and tails are connected into a through going surface. At this stage, pressure solution tails associated to the fault form at the asperities along the fault plane. Slip, with an opposite sense of shear respect to the first forming fault, is resolved on the pressure solution tails and a second set of faults with associated higher order pressure solution tails develop in the same remote stress field.\r\n\r\nThe resulting fault zone architecture depends on lithology and amount of slip. We distinguish small faults (slip of a few cm up to 1 m), intermediate faults (slip between 1 and 10 m) and large fault zones (slip >10 m), each with different associated structures. Large fault zones are characterized by a fault core consisting of cemented gouge and breccia with low permeability that would inhibit cross flow, whereas small and intermediate offset faults do not inhibit cross flow since they are characterized by a damage zone made up of slip surfaces and pressure solution seams that are conductive to fault-parallel and fault-normal flow. Deformation is more localized in calcarenite than in marl.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
